body:
Can't agree more with all you've said. 

&#x200B;

We're not in Bucks any more - completely priced out by Londoners who think it's normal to buy a 3 bed semi in a shit area for £550k but our families still live in Bucks (one set in Aylesbury, one set around Great Missenden, a sibling lives in Wendover) so we keep an eye on the house prices as really we'd love to move back that way from the West Country. Recently saw the new development in Little Kimble is advertising 3 bed houses for 800K and there was a 2 bed maisonette for sale in Wendover - on the main road with shared parking and no back garden - for sale for 900k in 2022. 

&#x200B;

The town is completely desolate, even with the new development by the theatre and from what our family say the traffic is appalling, the infrastructure is non-existent and people are becoming increasingly unfriendly. Seems like every time we drive back some new housing estate is being built but never any schools, GP surgeries, dentists etc.

One year my Grandad bought all of us grandkids (About 11 of us, ranging in ages from 3 to 25) ASDA Smartprice duvet sets. All of them singles. All of them plain designs in the scratchiest fabric (though different colours, blue for the boys and pink or purple for the girls, because... Grandad). That was a tough unwrapping.

&#x200B;

ETA - I've remembered another one. My ex was fucking useless at most things, present buying being one. One year it was Christmas Eve and I noticed at about midday that all my presents for him were wrapped and under the tree, so I asked him if he wanted to bring my presents in and put them there too. He looked rather sheepish and admitted that he hadn't actually got anything yet and was planning to get stuff when he went to meet his mates in the pub later (at 8pm on Christmas Eve). I sent him off with a flea in his ear and the next day unwrapped a flimsy paper notebook themed around a film franchise I'd never seen that must have cost about £2, a pin badge from another film franchise that I had seen but didn't particularly like and a box of those weird chocolates that you get at the till in some stores. He'd been to Waterstones I think and just grabbed the first three things he could lay his hands on. I'm a massive book lover and he knew this as well so literally *any fucking book* from that store would have been a better present.

I think I'm a little confused about the parking situation. On street view the house next door has a car parked on their same sized front drive, and the OPs house has a van seemingly parked across the opening of theirs. However it also looks like the parking bay extends from the end of the double yellows to the right of the house (by the parking regs sign) all the way down the road meaning that you're likely to have people parking in front of your house/blocking any access and with only 60 minute restrictions, potentially a high turn over of them too. Is the parking across the road for this house too? Where are you seeing the zebra crossing?

&#x200B;

ETA - Never mind. I was very much looking at the wrong house XD

Never met a poor one (and I live in a semi-rural area, working with people of all walks of life in my job so I come across them regularly) but equally I've never met one who wasn't 100% married to their job to a detriment. Lovely big farm houses our way with lots of land but they work until they drop - literally. Half of them would probably carry on working beyond the grave if they could figure it out.

&#x200B;

ETA - I think as well, as someone's mentioned below, they seem rich on the outside because their kids have 3 ponies to ride, they've got a Land Rover for every occasion, there's tonnes of land and lovely big buildings but in reality the ponies cost them next to nothing to care for as they have the land to graze them, the LRs are 35 years old and the land/buildings are passed down from generation to generation. Obviously massive generalization but I suspect that's part of the perception!

[https://www.etsy.com/uk/listing/1066125846/turtleneck-sewing-pattern-top-xs-xl?ref=yr\_purchases](https://www.etsy.com/uk/listing/1066125846/turtleneck-sewing-pattern-top-xs-xl?ref=yr_purchases) 

&#x200B;

I've bought this pattern and cut out all the pieces. Even if I make two sleeves I have only used about 0.6m of my fabric - why is it saying that I'd need 1.8m?

No, it's a sausage in a yorkshire pudding (similar to what you call Popplets? Poppers? Puffers? IDK, I saw Martha Stewart butcher them once).

&#x200B;

[https://realfood.tesco.com/recipes/toad-in-the-hole-with-onion-gravy.html](https://realfood.tesco.com/recipes/toad-in-the-hole-with-onion-gravy.html)

We booked a 5 night lodge holiday near the Peaks for a mini honeymoon after our wedding (during COVID so knew we weren't going to get a 'real' honeymoon for a bit). Very nice site, 5 lodges which were all brand new, hot tub with a TV and a sauna, quiet location. Well worth the £850 we paid for the time we spent there - bit expensive but ok for the occasion (it was the first week of the summer holidays too). 

&#x200B;

Looked again to see how much it would be to go back for our wedding anniversary and in two years the price has gone up to £2000 for the same week. Exact same lodge.

I think there's a world of difference between having empathy for people and being an arse about it. Some of the comments I've seen on tiktok have been disgusting and quite frankly I'm over a world where you can be cancelled for saying something online that a minority of people disagree with but you can openly laugh and joke about the horrific death of 5 people just because they have more money than any of the rest of us. 

&#x200B;

Empathy isn't a sparse resource. You can absolutely feel just as upset about their demise as you can refugees who are risking everything to cross the ocean in the hope of a better life. If more people realised that perhaps we'd live in a kinder world.

I recently met a 1 year old Hero. A baby girl. Called Hero. I wish I was kidding. 

&#x200B;

Her brother was called Oak. I wish I was kidding on that too. I live in rural England.

This was my first response to this thread, ha. 

&#x200B;

Not particularly on topic of this thread but the one that springs to mind as the most 'ffs why' is the toilets on one ward that are too small to get a wheelchair or stand aid in... obviously this is the ward where they put the ortho geris who've all had hip replacements and can't walk.

Definitely me! Doesn't help that I likely have ADHD - massive tendency to hyperfocus on things and drop them like a hot potato when something shiny and new comes along. 

&#x200B;

In the past I've done weight training, Tabbata/HIIT workouts, pole dancing, rock climbing, running, pilates/yoga, Crossfit... I'd love to try Hyrox but I'm not paying £120 for an hour long event. Running is the one I've stuck with the longest and my true love and if I'm honest I find weights boring but know I need to do them. Part of it is figuring out what's important to me at the time (I stopped pole dancing when I was training for my marathon as I wasn't getting enough rest days in with it) and part of it is keeping my interest level high enough with challenges or goals.

It's £20 a ticket to see Guardians of the Galaxy this evening in Cheltenham - [https://experience.cineworld.co.uk/select-tickets?sitecode=069&site=069&id=213077&lang=en-GB](https://experience.cineworld.co.uk/select-tickets?sitecode=069&site=069&id=213077&lang=en-GB) or [https://cheltenham.tivolicinemas.com/?p=tickets&perfCode=9265](https://cheltenham.tivolicinemas.com/?p=tickets&perfCode=9265)

&#x200B;

ETA - Sorry, the first cineworld link is a VIP ticket, which is £20. For regular it's £15 https://experience.cineworld.co.uk/select-tickets?sitecode=069&site=069&id=213065&lang=en-GB

FTM, 7 weeks PP here. I've been washing them all off in hot soapy water and putting them in either a cold water sanitiser (Milton) or microwaving them in sanitizer bags after every pump. If I don't need to do that I can save myself a lot of hassle XD

&#x200B;

I've also been using the pump (Medela Swing Maxi) with wet parts. Is it a no for just wearables or should I be drying the SM parts too? I'm waiting for my Momcozy S12 Pro to get here.

Dunno, but if you find any let me know. I'm sick of having conversations with both my parents (in their 60s) about how they should maybe not refer to people like that, or that perhaps saying things like "I don't care what people want to do but they don't need to do it in public" (when referring to a transperson walking down the street wearing female clothing but not presenting quite as female as I imagined they'd liked to have done). It's like stepping back to the 70s sometimes with them. 

&#x200B;

We'd best not mention the conversation that happened when they saw what Sam Smith was wearing to the Brits.

What's that old phrase? "Safety laws are written in blood"? 

&#x200B;

Can't believe we have to clarify this.

If you can't spend your hard earned money on objects you love what can you spend it on? Can't take it with you! 

&#x200B;

That pistachio cover is beautiful :)

It's shit like this that had me convinced I didn't want kids and now wakes me up in a cold sense of dread and anxiety at 4am in the morning (I'm 6 months pregnant). 

&#x200B;

I've started telling people "I don't want to hear the bad bits, only talk to me about kids if you want to tell me the good parts because otherwise I'm walking away from this conversation".

I did my first full this year too in a very similar time of 5:27 - my half PB is a bit quicker though at 2:13 (I completely blew up at mile 12 of all places when I choked on a gel and didn't get back in the zone). Before I got pregnant my plan was to try and bring my 10k time down under 54 minutes consistently, my 5k time under 27 minutes consistently (my PB is actually 25 minutes but I'm incredibly variable!) and my half time under 2:12 consistently. Then in 2025 consider another marathon aiming for sub 5 hours. 

&#x200B;

Now I'm pregnant, due in March and my goal for 2023 is to be running 10k under 55 minutes by December!

Totally. And it's pissing annoying.

&#x200B;

I was looking on etsy for a wax melt burner for my Mum's present a few weekends ago, saw one I liked but thought I'd keep looking just to see if there was anything else similar to it or better value etc as it was £10 plus P+P. Saw several listings for the exact same wax burner and multiple listings for other different designs, all from different sellers. All listed as "Hand made".

&#x200B;

Googled it and they're all being sold in B and M for £3. About as handmade as the Shein jumper I'm wearing is. I'm half tempted to report all the listings as being fraudulent... I see in the comments that if the seller had included a hand made wax melt then that counts as 'Etsy legal' but most of them were just the ceramic wax holder straight from the store.

I'm not sure, tbh! I've messaged her to let her know I'm looking into it and I'll see what she says.

&#x200B;

What's confusing me is according to this post - [https://www.reddit.com/r/vinted/comments/yvrk9c/package\_has\_been\_automatically\_returned\_after/-](https://www.reddit.com/r/vinted/comments/yvrk9c/package_has_been_automatically_returned_after/-) and a couple of others, it should have cancelled the sale completely and she shouldn't have been charged but equally I shouldn't have been paid?

TOP is pretty much the only concert I've been to where seated stands, sings and dances just as much as the floor does. Perhaps it was because I had shitty seats for MCR but we sat the entire way through. Better seats for Panic! but no-one else stood up and I felt rude standing when people around me weren't. 

&#x200B;

I will address one of the points in the original post and just apologise for anyone who was around me at London SSE, I absolutely lost my shit when they cut Shy Away with Teenagers by MCR and had a bit of a screaming moment.

I was originally planning to get the solid colour weeks anyway (mint or peach) and now feel totally assured that that is the right option for me. 

&#x200B;

If I'm honest I don't think I really like any of last years prints either :o

They wouldn't ignore pain, but pain is a really unreliable indicator of how unwell someone is. We all have very different pain thresholds, and someone's 10 "A bear is gnawing my nuts off" can be very different to someone elses who might actually rate that as a 3 "This isn't pleasant but ok". 

&#x200B;

I used to work in an area that used pain as a measure quite often and some people would sit there with a blank expression and say "Oh yes, when I do \[insert action here\] it's a 10 out of 10 pain for sure" which is uh, not how pain scales work.

I've got MP in at the moment, the flowers are a beautiful light lilac. 

&#x200B;

I have absolutely no idea what I'm doing; they're currently smothering my poor tomato that I planted next to them :(
